From: Henk Wiedig <hwiedig@googlemail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: artosyn: cvisp: hand frames back at depth 2

The CVISP block takes new plane bases at a tick for the frame after the
one starting then, so a buffer armed at tick k is written between ticks
k+1 and k+2 and is complete just before k+2.  Depth 3 handed it back at
k+3, one full frame period (16.7 ms at 1080p60) after the frame was in
memory; depth 1 hands it back as the write starts, which is the cause of
the torn frames the old comment attributes to a still-draining DMA.

Measured on a P1 with a probe that marks rows at the top, middle and
bottom of the luma plane and the last row of both chroma planes before
QBUF and watches every queued buffer for the overwrite:

  arm tick -> top row      +16.8 ms
           -> middle row   +24.9 ms
           -> last Y/U/V   +32.6 ms (0 to 1.6 ms before tick k+2)

At depth 2, 0 of 598 buffers saw a write after hand-back (each held
20 ms); at depth 1 all of them did, 14-15 ms into the hold.  On the
air unit's video path at depth 2: 0 CVISP drops, 0 camera skips.

The buffer timestamp is the hand-back time, so a capture-to-display
figure built on it never showed the extra frame.

Applied on top of the MissingLynk ml-kernel overlay.

---
--- a/drivers/media/artosyn/ar-cvisp.c
+++ b/drivers/media/artosyn/ar-cvisp.c
@@ -197,32 +197,30 @@
 /*
  * Frame ticks a buffer is held before it is handed back.
  *
- * The tick is the ISP's statistics event, so the output DMA for a frame is not
- * known to have drained when it fires. At 2 a
- * buffer is held a further full frame period; the cost is a buffer, and the
- * hardware holds one per unit of depth out of a pool of five.
+ * Plane bases written at a tick do not take the frame that starts with it:
+ * that one is already going to the buffer armed a tick earlier. A buffer armed
+ * at tick k is written during the next frame, between ticks k+1 and k+2.
+ * Measured on a P1 at 1080p60 by marking a stretch of the top, middle and last
+ * luma rows and the last row of each chroma plane before QBUF and watching all
+ * queued buffers for the overwrite: top +16.8 ms, middle +24.9 ms, the last
+ * rows of all three planes +32.6 ms after the arming tick (median; 0 to 1.6 ms
+ * ahead of tick k+2, the probe polling at under 1 ms).
  *
- * Measured at both settings with ml-v4l2grab -m, which fills a buffer with a
- * position-keyed pattern before queueing it and reports what survived: at 1
- * and at 2 alike a completed buffer carries 518400 luma words and 138240 per
- * chroma plane, its full written extent, with no marker left at the bottom.
- * The write has drained by the next tick.
+ * So 2 hands a buffer back as soon as it is written: over 598 frames held for
+ * 20 ms each, no write landed in one after hand-back. 1 hands it back at the
+ * tick the block starts writing it; a consumer reading it then, as the wave5
+ * encoder does through dmabuf, gets torn frames while the drop counter stays
+ * clean.
+ * 3, the original default, holds a finished frame one more period, 16.7 ms at
+ * 60 fps, for nothing. The hardware holds one buffer per unit of depth out of
+ * a pool of five.
  *
- * That measures the DMA, not the consumer. A consumer that still holds the
- * buffer when the next tick re-arms it reads while the hardware writes: the
- * wave5 encoder imports these buffers by dmabuf and does exactly that, so at
- * depth 1 it encodes torn frames while the drop counter stays clean. The
- * default is therefore 3, which is what the recorder and the air-unit video
- * path are validated at; 1 remains selectable for the drain measurement above
- * and costs a buffer less out of the pool of five.
- *
- * Take the measurement from a buffer marked while the stream is running. See
- * the geometry block for what the first buffer of a stream reports instead.
+ * The timestamp is taken at hand-back, so at 2 it marks the end of the write.
  */
-static unsigned int depth = 3;
+static unsigned int depth = 2;
 module_param(depth, uint, 0444);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(depth,
-		 "frame ticks a buffer is held before completion, 1 to 4 (default 3)");
+		 "frame ticks a buffer is held before completion, 1 to 4 (default 2)");
 
 /*
  * Bring the sensor, input path and ISP up from STREAMON, so opening the node is
